    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-4">US B1/B2 Visa from Pakistan — Step by Step</h1>
    <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mb-8">Last updated: October 2026 · 9 minute read</p>

    <section class="prose max-w-none">
      <p class="lead text-lg">
        The US B1/B2 visa is a combined business and tourism nonimmigrant visa, and the most common route Pakistani travellers take to visit the United States for short stays. This guide covers the full process — from DS-160 to interview.
      </p>

      <h2>B1 vs B2 — The Difference</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>B1 — Business Visitor:</strong> conferences, business meetings, contract negotiations, training, settling estates. No employment or paid work.</li>
        <li><strong>B2 — Tourism/Pleasure:</strong> tourism, visiting family or friends, medical treatment, participating in social events.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For most Pakistani applicants, the embassy issues a <strong>combined B1/B2 visa</strong> that covers both purposes. You don't need to pick one.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 1 — Complete DS-160</h2>
      <p>
        DS-160 is the online nonimmigrant visa application. Fill it carefully at <code>ceac.state.gov/genniv</code>. The form takes 60–90 minutes if you have information ready.
      </p>
      <p>
        You'll need: passport details, travel history (last 5 years), employment history (last 5 years), education history, family details (parents, spouse, children), social media handles used in the last 5 years, and purpose of visit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Save frequently. Mistakes cannot be edited after submission — you'd need to start over. Print the confirmation page with barcode and keep it for your interview.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 2 — Pay the MRV Fee</h2>
      <p>
        The Machine Readable Visa fee is <strong>$185</strong> (approximately PKR 52,000). Pay through designated Pakistani banks or online at <code>ustraveldocs.com/pk</code>. The receipt is required to book your interview.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 3 — Schedule Your Interview</h2>
      <p>
        Interviews are held at:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>US Embassy Islamabad</strong></li>
        <li><strong>US Consulate Karachi</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Visit <code>ustraveldocs.com/pk</code>, create an account, and book your appointment. Interview wait times can vary enormously — anywhere from a few weeks to over a year for first-time applicants. Check both posts for the earliest available slot.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 4 — Prepare Supporting Documents</h2>
      <p>
        While many applicants aren't asked to show documents at the interview, bringing them is essential. The officer may ask for proof during the interview.
      </p>
      <h3>Essential documents:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Current passport (valid 6+ months beyond intended stay)</li>
        <li>All previous passports</li>
        <li>DS-160 confirmation page</li>
        <li>MRV fee receipt</li>
        <li>Interview appointment letter</li>
        <li>Recent 2x2 inch photo (white background, head size 1 to 1 3/8 inches)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Evidence of ties to Pakistan:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Bank statements — 6 months</li>
        <li>Property ownership documents</li>
        <li>Employment letter + salary slips</li>
        <li>Business registration (if self-employed)</li>
        <li>Tax returns — last 2 years</li>
        <li>Family details (spouse, children, parents)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Purpose of trip:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Hotel bookings or invitation letter from US host</li>
        <li>Host's immigration status (visa, green card, I-94)</li>
        <li>Flight itinerary (reservation, not purchased)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Step 5 — The Interview</h2>
      <p>
        The interview lasts <strong>2–5 minutes</strong>. The officer has already reviewed your DS-160 before you approach the window. They decide quickly based on your answers, demeanour, and documents.
      </p>

      <h3>Common Questions:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What is the purpose of your trip to the US?</li>
        <li>Where will you stay?</li>
        <li>How long will you stay?</li>
        <li>Who is paying for your trip?</li>
        <li>What do you do for work?</li>
        <li>Have you traveled before? Where?</li>
        <li>Do you have family in the US?</li>
        <li>Are you married? Do you have children?</li>
        <li>What property do you own in Pakistan?</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>How to Answer</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Be brief and direct.</strong> One or two sentences per answer. Don't volunteer extra information.</li>
        <li><strong>Be honest.</strong> Lying leads to lifetime bans.</li>
        <li><strong>Make eye contact.</strong> Confident, calm demeanour.</li>
        <li><strong>Have facts ready.</strong> Dates, costs, addresses — don't fumble.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Section 214(b) — The Most Common Refusal</h2>
      <p>
        Under US immigration law, every nonimmigrant visa applicant is presumed to be an intending immigrant until proven otherwise. You must demonstrate <strong>strong ties to Pakistan</strong> that will compel your return after the visit.
      </p>
      <p>
        A 214(b) refusal means the officer wasn't convinced of your intent to return. It's not a permanent ban — you can reapply after your circumstances change (new job, marriage, property purchase, travel history to other countries). Reapplying immediately without changes usually results in another refusal.
      </p>

      <h3>Strong Ties Include:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Permanent employment with longer tenure</li>
        <li>Property ownership in Pakistan</li>
        <li>Dependant spouse and children remaining in Pakistan</li>
        <li>Business ownership</li>
        <li>Elderly parents to care for</li>
        <li>Previous travel to other Western countries with timely return</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Visa Duration</h2>
      <p>
        For Pakistani passport holders, approved B1/B2 visas are typically valid for <strong>5 years with multiple entries</strong>. However, each entry allows a stay of up to <strong>6 months maximum</strong>, as determined by the CBP officer at the port of entry — not the visa validity.
      </p>

      <h2>Visa Interview Waiver (Dropbox)</h2>
      <p>
        Pakistani applicants who have previously held a US visa in the same category may be eligible to renew without an interview through the Interview Waiver (also called "Dropbox"). Eligibility criteria change — check <code>ustraveldocs.com/pk</code> for current requirements.
      </p>

      <h2>Travel on an Approved Visa</h2>
      <p>
        A US visa allows you to travel to a US port of entry and request admission. Final admission is granted by a CBP officer at the airport. They determine how long you can stay (typically 6 months for B1/B2). Carry the same supporting documents in your hand luggage — officers may ask questions on arrival.
      </p>

      <h2>Related Tools</h2>
      <p>
